  "worked_solution": "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\omega t+\u03d5),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $\u03d5$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,\u27f9\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t+\u03d5).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5=0\\,\u27f9\\,\u03d5=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=2.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=2R\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=2R\\,\u27f9\\,R=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $1\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t.\n$$",
